  {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_008fd3b2_4013_451f_8827_52bceab11841",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The class for entities where an interpreter, interpretant, reference and sign relate with each others semiotically."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/metrology#"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Semiosis"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf": [
      {
        "@id": "_:n6a0b4d77db1d4bb4a28be148935c0b69b4699"
      },
      {
        "@id": "_:n6a0b4d77db1d4bb4a28be148935c0b69b4700"
      },
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_8bb6b688_812a_4cb9_b76c_d5a058928719"
      },
      {
        "@id": "_:n6a0b4d77db1d4bb4a28be148935c0b69b4701"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Me looking a cat and saying loud: \"Cat!\" -> the semiosis process\n\nme -> interpreter\ncat -> object (in Peirce semiotics)\nthe cat perceived by my mind -> interpretant\n\"Cat!\" -> sign, the produced sign"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Semiosis"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_8a137e9f_579c_4e28_baca_e8980eb0c3db": [
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "EquivalentTo: Deduction or Declaration or Cognition"
      },
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "EquivalentTo: FullSemiosis or PartialSemiosis"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The class for entities where an interpreter, interpretant, reference and sign relate with each others semiotically."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Semiosis is intended here as a 4D entity, meaning that is not necessarily intended as a process. This enables users to represent more general state of things without necessarily committing to a process/object classification (which in a 4D framework is subjective, and not ontological) through the persistence perspective (in fact semiotics module is independent from persistence module).\nFor example, in a deduction (semiosis) the yellow tinted skin (index) of a patient is a symptom for liver desease (referent) according to a doctor (interpreter). If the user wants to include a commit towards persistency then the deduction can be either:\n- an obejct: the deduction is a system (object) and the doctor, skin and patient are object components\n-  a process: where the deduction is a process and the doctor, skin and patient are participants\nSince in the EMMO the object/process categorisation is relation-driven and object/process are not disjointed, the abovementioned deduction can be seen both as a process or object depending on the relation used by the user to navigate the graph."
      }
    ]
  },
