  {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#Angstrom",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The Angstr`o`m is an internationally recognized unit of length equal to 0.1  nanometre or 1 × 10⁻¹⁰ metres.  Although accepted for use, it is not formally defined within the International System of Units(SI). The angstrom is often used in the natural sciences to express the sizes of atoms, lengths of chemical bonds and the wavelengths of electromagnetic radiation, and in technology for the dimensions of parts of integrated circuits. It is also commonly used in structural biology.\n\n-- QUDT"
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Ångström is not mentioned in the SI system and deprecated by the International Bureau of Weights and Measures (BIPM).\n\nDispite of that, it is often used in the natural sciences and technology."
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Measure of length defined as 1e-10 metres."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
