  {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_e04884d9_eda6_487e_93d5_7722d7eda96b",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The union of the object or process classes."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/metrology#"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Persistence"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_49267eba_5548_4163_8f36_518d65b583f9"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Persistence"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_8a137e9f_579c_4e28_baca_e8980eb0c3db": [
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "EquivalentTo: Process or Object"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The union of the object or process classes."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The persistence perspective classifies entities according to the persistence of temporal parts of specifc type, introducing the distiction between objects (when a type is conserved through all its time parts) or processes (where a type is not conserved through all its time parts).\nThis perspective provides classes aimed to represent concepts similar to the ones of endurant and perdurant (a.k.a. continuant and occurrent). However, in the EMMO case, an entity is defined as an object or a process according to a specific type, and not per-se. For this reason an entity in the EMMO can be classified both object or a process depending on the reference type, due to the underlying 4D framework (e.g. an individual may belong both to the class of runners and the class of running process), and the distinction is purely representational.\nThis categorisation has been introduced due to the large use of object/process commonsensical concepts, and the fact that our language distinguish between nouns and verbs to address things, forcing the separation between things that happens and things that persist."
      }
    ]
  },
