      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_8a137e9f_579c_4e28_baca_e8980eb0c3db": [
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "EquivalentTo: PhysicalQuantity or OrdinalQuantity"
      },
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "DisjointUnionOf: SubjectiveProperty, ObjectiveProperty"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A quantifiable property of a phenomenon, body, or substance."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_bb49844b_45d7_4f0d_8cae_8e552cbc20d6": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "quantity"
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "measurand"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "VIM defines a quantity as a \"property of a phenomenon, body, or substance, where the property has a magnitude that can be expressed as a number and a reference\".\n\nA quantity in EMMO is a property and therefore only addresses the first part of the VIM definition (that is a property of a phenomenon, body, or substance). The second part (that it can be expressed as a number and a reference) is syntactic and addressed by emmo:QuantityValue."
      }
    ]
  },
