  {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_7286b164_df4c_4c14_a4b5_d41ad9c121f3",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A conventional that stands for an object according to a code of interpretation to which the interpreter refers."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/isq#"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Coded"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_35d2e130_6e01_41ed_94f7_00b333d46cf9"
      },
      {
        "@id": "_:n6a0b4d77db1d4bb4a28be148935c0b69b4288"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A biography that makes use of a code that is provided by the meaning of the element of the language used by the author."
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The name \"red\" that stands for the color of an object."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Coded"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_8a137e9f_579c_4e28_baca_e8980eb0c3db": [
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "EquivalentTo: Description or Property"
      },
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "EquivalentTo: Observed or Estimated"
      },
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "EquivalentTo: Subjective or Objective"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A conventional that stands for an object according to a code of interpretation to which the interpreter refers."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A conventional referring to an object according to a specific code that reflects the results of a specific interaction mechanism and is shared between other interpreters.\nA coded is always a partial representation of an object since it reflects the object capability to be part of a specific determination.\nA coded is a sort of name or label that we put upon objects that interact with an determiner in the same specific way.\n\nFor example, \"hot\" objects are objects that interact with an observer through a perception mechanism aimed to perceive an heat source. The code is made of terms such as \"hot\", \"warm\", \"cold\", that commonly refer to the perception of heat."
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Let's define the class Colour as the subclass of the coded signs that involve photon emission and electromagnetic radiation sensible observers.\nAn individual C of this class Colour can be defined be declaring the process individual (e.g. daylight illumination) and the observer (e.g. my eyes)\nStating that an entity E hasCoded C, we mean that it can be observed by such setup of process + observer (i.e. observed by my eyes under daylight).\nThis definition can be specialised for human eye perception, so that the observer can be a generic human, or to camera perception so that the observer can be a device.\nThis can be used in material characterization, to define exactly the type of measurement done, including the instrument type."
      }
    ]
  },
