  {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_a1083d0a_c1fb_471f_8e20_a98f881ad527",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The class of individuals that stand for an elementary mark of a specific symbolic code (alphabet)."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/metrology#"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Symbol"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_1141d225_fe90_4230_82c6_31cab6a4e3fe"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "AlphabeticEntity"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The class of letter \"A\" is the symbol as idea and the letter A that you see on the screen is the mark that can be represented by an individual belonging to \"A\"."
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A letter, a number, a punctuation sign, a space, an UTF-8 character."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Symbol"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The class of individuals that stand for an elementary mark of a specific symbolic code (alphabet)."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Symbols of a formal language need not be symbols of anything. For instance there are logical constants which do not refer to any idea, but rather serve as a form of punctuation in the language (e.g. parentheses).\n\nSymbols of a formal language must be capable of being specified without any reference to any interpretation of them.\n(Wikipedia)"
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Subclasses of 'Symbol' are alphabets, in formal languages terminology. A 'Symbol' is atomic for that alphabet, i.e. it has no parts that are symbols for the same alphabet.\ne.g. a math symbol is not made of other math symbols\nA Symbol may be a String in another language.\ne.g. \"Bq\" is the symbol for Becquerel units when dealing with metrology, or a string of \"B\" and \"q\" symbols when dealing with characters."
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The class is the idea of the symbol, while the individual of that class stands for a specific mark (or token) of that idea."
      }
    ]
  },
