        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Geometrical"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_3e7add3d_e6ed_489a_a796_8e31fef9b490"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A geometrical object can be expressed in many different forms.\n\nFor example, a line can be expressed by:\na) an equation like y=mx+q, which is both an 'equation' and a 'geometrical'\nb) a line drawn with a pencil on a paper, which is simply a 'graphical' object\nc) a set of axioms, when the properties of a line are inferred by the interpreter reading them, that are both 'graphical' and also 'formula'\n\nThe case a) is a geometrical and mathematical, b) is geometrical and pictorial, while c) is geometrical and a composition of idiomatic strings."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Geometrical"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A 'graphical' aimed to represent a geometrical concept."
      }
    ]
