  {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_3e7add3d_e6ed_489a_a796_8e31fef9b490",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A causal object whose properties variation are encoded by an agent and that can be decoded by another agent according to a specific rule."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/isq#"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Data"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_1e877c70_3b01_45a8_a8f6_8ce4f6a24660"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_5a10e288_c6a5_409a_a16a_98a2fb8be4f3"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "EncodedContrast"
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "EncodedData"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A signal through a cable. A sound wave. Words on a page. The pattern of excited states within a computer RAM."
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A Radio Morse Code transmission can be addressed by combination of perspectives.\nPhysicalistic: the electromagnetic pulses can be defined as individual A (of type Field) and the strip of paper coming out a printer receiver can be defined as individual B (of type Matter).\nData: both A and B are also DiscreteData class individuals. In particular they may belong to a MorseData class, subclass of DiscreteData.\nPerceptual: B is an individual belonging to the graphical entities expressing symbols. In particular is a formula under the MorseLanguage class, made of a combination of . and - symbols.\nSemiotics: A and B can be signs if they refers to something else (e.g. a report about a fact, names)."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Data"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A causal object whose properties variation are encoded by an agent and that can be decoded by another agent according to a specific rule."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "We call \"decoding\" the act of recognise the variation according to a particular rule and generate another equivalent schema (e.g. in the agent's cognitive apparatus, as another form of data).\nWe call \"interpreting\" the act of providing semantic meaning to data, which is covered by the semiotic perspective."
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Variations in data are generated by an agent (not necessarily human) and are intended to be decoded by the same or another agent using the same encoding rules.\nData are always generated by an agent but not necessarily possess a semantic meaning, either because it's lost or unknown or because simply they possess none (e.g. a random generation of symbols). A data object may be used as the physical basis for a sign, under Semiotics perspective."
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "EncodedData is the class where inventions that don't actually exist would be placed.\nFor example, the blueprint of a bridge is an encoded data. When the bridge will be built, than the blueprint will also be a semiotical entity.\nAnother example is Superman, that exists only as a set of magazines, drawings, novels, movies, but not in the real world."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d": [
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI",
        "@value": "https://no.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data"
      }
    ]
  },
