  {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_057e7d57_aff0_49de_911a_8861d85cef40",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A discrete data whose elements can be decoded as tokens from one or more alphabets, without necessarily respecting syntactic rules."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/isq#"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Symbolic"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_be8592a7_68d1_4a06_ad23_82f2b56ef926"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "fe780\nemmo\n!5*a\ncat\nfor(i=0;i<N;++i)"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Symbolic"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_8a137e9f_579c_4e28_baca_e8980eb0c3db": [
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "EquivalentTo: SymbolicConstruct or Symbol"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A discrete data whose elements can be decoded as tokens from one or more alphabets, without necessarily respecting syntactic rules."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A symbolic entity is not necessarily graphical (e.g. it doesn't necessarily have the physical shape of a letter), but its elements can be decoded and put in relation with an alphabet.\nIn other words, a sequence of bit \"1000010\" in a RAM (a non-graphical entity) is a valid symbol since it can be decoded through ASCII rules as the letter \"B\". The same holds for an entity standing for the sound of a voice saying: \"Hello\", since it can be decomposed in discrete parts, each of them being associated to a letter of an alphabet."
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A symbolic object possesses a reductionistic oriented structure.\nFor example, text is made of words, spaces and punctuations. Words are made of characters (i.e. atomic symbols)."
      }
    ]
  },
