  {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_d67ee67e_4fac_4676_82c9_aec361dba698",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#IrreflexiveProperty",
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "We say that an entity causes another if there is a quantum part of the first that is in causal relation with a quantum parts of the second.\nMore formally, causality is defined as a relation between individuals x and y that holds if and only if:\na) x having a part that is causing an effect on a part of y\nb) x and y are non-overlapping\nAn entity cannot cause itself or a part of itself. For this reasons causality between entities excludes reflexivity and prevents them to overlap.\nThe existence of one-directional of bi-directional causality relations between two entities is what reconstruct time-like and space-like relations, respectively."
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A relation between an entity that causally affects another."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo/foundation/mereocausality#"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "isCauseOf"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subPropertyOf": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_ad0e72fc_dcaa_490d_8371_b4d814dcda2c"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "causes"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example": [
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "John yesterday is the cause of John today, is an example of temporal-like causality. The desk supporting my laptop is a space-like causality."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "isCauseOf"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_31252f35_c767_4b97_a877_1235076c3e13": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "We say that an entity causes another if there is a quantum part of the first that is in causal relation with a quantum parts of the second.\nMore formally, causality is defined as a relation between individuals x and y that holds if and only if:\na) x having a part that is causing an effect on a part of y\nb) x and y are non-overlapping\nAn entity cannot cause itself or a part of itself. For this reasons causality between entities excludes reflexivity and prevents them to overlap.\nThe existence of one-directional of bi-directional causality relations between two entities is what reconstruct time-like and space-like relations, respectively."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A relation between an entity that causally affects another."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Each pair of causally connected entities is either in isDirectCauseOf or isIndirectCauseOf relation. The two are mutually exclusive."
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The OWL 2 DL version of the EMMO introduces this object property as primitive causal relation. It refers to the macro causality relation mC(x,y), defined in the EMMO FOL version.\nWhile the EMMO FOL introduces the quantum causality relation C(x,y) as primitive, the OWL 2 DL version of the EMMO substantially simplifies the theory, neglecting these lower level relations that are well above DL expressivity.\nFor this reason the EMMO OWL 2 DL will only deal with macro causality relations, sacrificing the full FOL theory expressivity for the sake of simplicity."
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The cause relation applies to both quantums and macro-entities (entities made of more than one quantum). It is admissible for two entities to be one the cause of the other, excepts when they are both quantums."
      }
    ]
  },
