  {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_946b5162_95b0_4801_81d4_efef43842a0d",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The superclass for all classes of individuals that requires their individuals to be part of another specific class of entity of different type with a specific role with respect to it."
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The qualified role concept express the requirement for an entity of a type, say A, to be part of a larger entity of another type, say B, with A and B belonging to different taxonomical branches.\nThe concept of role is then defined as something that needs a wider and specific environment to exist.\nFor example, the car radio type requires all instances to be a part of a car, while the opposite (the car having a radio) is not mandatory for qualified roles.  The same applies for airplane winglets that are by definition an optional part of an airplane. Food preservatives are anothe optional part of foods."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo/reference/workflow#"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "QualifiedRole"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_0277f24a_ea7f_4917_81b7_fb0406c8fc62"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "QRole"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "QualifiedRole"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_31252f35_c767_4b97_a877_1235076c3e13": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The qualified role concept express the requirement for an entity of a type, say A, to be part of a larger entity of another type, say B, with A and B belonging to different taxonomical branches.\nThe concept of role is then defined as something that needs a wider and specific environment to exist.\nFor example, the car radio type requires all instances to be a part of a car, while the opposite (the car having a radio) is not mandatory for qualified roles.  The same applies for airplane winglets that are by definition an optional part of an airplane. Food preservatives are anothe optional part of foods."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The superclass for all classes of individuals that requires their individuals to be part of another specific class of entity of different type with a specific role with respect to it."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "We usually have the same word to identify both the role and the name of a device. for example, under the term hammer we can identify both a product and generic entity used for hammering a nail.\nThe concept of role helps to distinguish between these two concepts. Ideally ther shuld be an HammerRole and an HammerProduct types. The product may or may not be used as HammerRole, but only expresses the fact that it has been designed as an optimal hammering device. The hammering role can be played by any hard-enough object, even if it is not an hammer product."
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Al QRole subclasses shuld have an axiom to express the role requirements, in the form of:\nMyQRoleClass\n    SubClassOf: isProperPartOf some AnotherClass\nThis is a necessary but not sufficient condition."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_8785be5a_2493_4b12_8f39_31907ab11748",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#SymmetricProperty",
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#IrreflexiveProperty",
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "An interaction that is the sum of direct causality relations between two entities that are interpretable as fundamental physical interactions."
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A spatial contact between two entities occurs when the two entities are in an interaction relation whose causal structure is a representation of the fundamental interactions between elementary particles (Feynman diagrams).\nIt means that if two entities are in contact, then there is at least a couple of elementary particles, one part of the first and one part of the second, interacting according to one of the fundamental interactions through virtual particles. This kind of connection is space-like (i.e. interconnecting force carrier particle is offshelf).\nContacts between two entities exclude the possibility of other causal relations that are not included in a fundamental space-like interaction."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c5ddfdba_c074_4aa4_ad6b_1ac4942d300d"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo/foundation/mereocausality#"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "contacts"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c5ddfdba_c074_4aa4_ad6b_1ac4942d300d"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subPropertyOf": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_555d0261_da5e_4301_b7f9_46f604a32e91"
      },
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_11501e0a_4442_4bd0_8483_7ea6dc8ba444"
      },
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_54d0d0f4_d9fa_4179_a9b5_4110c49dafff"
      },
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_a69a215c_4f4d_4729_a9c6_39302bbc5f77"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#propertyDisjointWith": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_ec90a8f0_16bf_4f76_b378_ef69b587b426"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "adjacentWith"
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "touches"
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "hasSpatialnteractionWith"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "An elementary electron is in contact with another elementary electron in a scattering process."
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The laptop is in contact with the desk, since there is a double-directional causality."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "contacts"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_31252f35_c767_4b97_a877_1235076c3e13": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A spatial contact between two entities occurs when the two entities are in an interaction relation whose causal structure is a representation of the fundamental interactions between elementary particles (Feynman diagrams).\nIt means that if two entities are in contact, then there is at least a couple of elementary particles, one part of the first and one part of the second, interacting according to one of the fundamental interactions through virtual particles. This kind of connection is space-like (i.e. interconnecting force carrier particle is offshelf).\nContacts between two entities exclude the possibility of other causal relations that are not included in a fundamental space-like interaction."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "An interaction that is the sum of direct causality relations between two entities that are interpretable as fundamental physical interactions."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The contact relation is not an ordering relation since is symmetric."
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Spatial contact is symmetric and irreflexive."
      }
    ]
  },
