  {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_b803f122_4acb_4064_9d71_c1e5fd091fc9",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The class of individuals that stands for semiotic objects, i.e. objects that take part on a semiotic process."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/isq#"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "SemioticEntity"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_8bb6b688_812a_4cb9_b76c_d5a058928719"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "SemioticEntity"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_8a137e9f_579c_4e28_baca_e8980eb0c3db": [
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "EquivalentTo: Interpreter or SemioticObject or Sign"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The class of individuals that stands for semiotic objects, i.e. objects that take part on a semiotic process."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Semiotic subclasse are defined using Peirce's semiotic theory.\n\n\"Namely, a sign is something, A, which brings something, B, its interpretant sign determined or created by it, into the same sort of correspondence with something, C, its object, as that in which itself stands to C.\" (Peirce 1902, NEM 4, 20–21).\n\nThe triadic elements:\n- 'sign': the sign A (e.g. a name)\n- 'interpretant': the sign B as the effects of the sign A on the interpreter (e.g. the mental concept of what a name means)\n- 'object': the object C (e.g. the entity to which the sign A and B refer to)\n\nThis class includes also the 'interpeter' i.e. the entity that connects the 'sign' to the 'object'"
      }
    ]
  },
