    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "In physics, the Planck mass, denoted by mₚ, is the unit of mass in the system of natural units known as Planck units. It is defined so that ≈  1.2209 × 10 GeV/c₀ = 2.17651(13) × 10 kg, (or 21.7651 μ g), where c₀ is the speed of light in a vacuum, G is the gravitational constant, and ℏ is the reduced Planck constant. Particle physicists and cosmologists often use the reduced Planck mass, which is ≈  4.341 × 10 kg = 2.435  × 10 GeV/c. The added factor of 1/{√{8π}} simplifies a number of equations in general relativity. Quantum effects are typified by the magnitude of Planck's constant.\n\n-- QUDT"
      }
