  {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_3f9ae00e_810c_4518_aec2_7200e424cf68",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Q(x) ≡ ¬∃y(PP(y,x))"
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The class of the mereological and causal fundamental entities having no proper parts"
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A quantum is the most fundamental item of the EMMO (both mereologically and causally) and is considered causally self-connected by definition.\nThe quantum concept recalls the fact that there is lower epistemological limit to our knowledge of the universe, related to the uncertainity principle.\nIn the EMMO, space and time emerge following the network of causal connections between quantum objects. So quantum objects are adimensional objects, that precede space and time dimensions: they are simple beings (in greek οντα).\nUsing physics concepts, we can think the quantum as an elementary particle (e.g. an electron) in a specific state between two causal interactions. For a quanta to change its state, it requires an interaction.\nA quantum may be thought as standing for an incoming or outcoming real particle in a Feynman diagram in a scattering event."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo/foundation/mereocausality#"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Quantum"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_52b8a39b_e5ff_46d3_b972_65bc43077a07"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_53ed8611_2cd7_4286_9ef0_36572117b963"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "RealParticle"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "From a physics perspective a quantum can be related to smallest identifiable entities, according to the limits imposed by the uncertainty principle in space and time measurements.\nHowever, the quantum mereotopology approach is not restricted only to physics. For example, in a manpower management ontology, a quantum can stand for an hour (time) of a worker (space) activity."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Quantum"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_31252f35_c767_4b97_a877_1235076c3e13": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A quantum is the most fundamental item of the EMMO (both mereologically and causally) and is considered causally self-connected by definition.\nThe quantum concept recalls the fact that there is lower epistemological limit to our knowledge of the universe, related to the uncertainity principle.\nIn the EMMO, space and time emerge following the network of causal connections between quantum objects. So quantum objects are adimensional objects, that precede space and time dimensions: they are simple beings (in greek οντα).\nUsing physics concepts, we can think the quantum as an elementary particle (e.g. an electron) in a specific state between two causal interactions. For a quanta to change its state, it requires an interaction.\nA quantum may be thought as standing for an incoming or outcoming real particle in a Feynman diagram in a scattering event."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_70fe84ff_99b6_4206_a9fc_9a8931836d84": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Q(x) ≡ ¬∃y(PP(y,x))"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The class of the mereological and causal fundamental entities having no proper parts"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A quantum is the EMMO mereological atomistic and causal reductionistic entity.  To avoid confusion with the concept of atom coming from physics and to underline the causal reductionistic approach, we will use the expression quantum mereology, instead of atomistic mereology."
      }
    ]
  },
