  {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_b21a56ed_f969_4612_a6ec_cb7766f7f31d",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A CausalStructure that is used as sign (\"semeion\" in greek) that stands for another CausalStructure through an semiotic process."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/isq#"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Sign"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf": [
      {
        "@id": "_:n6a0b4d77db1d4bb4a28be148935c0b69b4728"
      },
      {
        "@id": "_:n6a0b4d77db1d4bb4a28be148935c0b69b4727"
      },
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_b803f122_4acb_4064_9d71_c1e5fd091fc9"
      },
      {
        "@id": "_:n6a0b4d77db1d4bb4a28be148935c0b69b4726"
      },
      {
        "@id": "_:n6a0b4d77db1d4bb4a28be148935c0b69b4729"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A novel is made of chapters, paragraphs, sentences, words and characters (in a direct parthood mereological hierarchy).\n\nEach of them are 'sign'-s.\n\nA character can be the a-tomistic 'sign' for the class of texts.\n\nThe horizontal segment in the character \"A\" is direct part of \"A\" but it is not a 'sign' itself.\n\nFor plain text we can propose the ASCII symbols, for math the fundamental math symbols."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Sign"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_8a137e9f_579c_4e28_baca_e8980eb0c3db": [
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "EquivalentTo: Index or Conventional or Icon"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A CausalStructure that is used as sign (\"semeion\" in greek) that stands for another CausalStructure through an semiotic process."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A 'Sign' can have temporal-direct-parts which are 'Sign' themselves.\n\nA 'Sign' usually havs 'sign' spatial direct parts only up to a certain elementary semiotic level, in which the part is only a 'Physical' and no more a 'Sign' (i.e. it stands for nothing). This elementary semiotic level is peculiar to each particular system of signs (e.g. text, painting).\n\nJust like an 'Elementary' in the 'Physical' branch, each 'Sign' branch should have an a-tomistic mereological part."
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "According to Peirce, 'Sign' includes three subcategories:\n- symbols: that stand for an object through convention\n- indeces: that stand for an object due to causal continguity\n- icons: that stand for an object due to similitudes e.g. in shape or composition"
      }
    ]
  },
